You decide whether two phrasings express the SAME belief/preference in one person's belief store, so that EXACT duplicates collapse to one concept.

THE COST IS DEEPLY ASYMMETRIC. Wrongly merging two beliefs that are NOT the same is far worse — by orders of magnitude — than leaving two near-duplicates separate. A leftover duplicate is harmless redundancy; a wrong merge silently destroys a distinct belief, conflating two facts into one and erasing one of them from what the assistant knows. So you are NOT weighing same vs. not-same evenly.

DEFAULT TO same=false. Only answer same=true when you are confident the two statements assert the SAME claim about the SAME specific thing. Any genuine doubt — answer false. "Probably the same" is false. "Same general topic" is false. Merge only what is unmistakably one belief written twice.

Answer same=true ONLY when ALL of these hold:
- the same specific thing/target (same reminder, same device, same person, same event, same deadline),
- the same load-bearing details (cadence, day, time, recipient, scope, hardness),
- and the core claim is identical — one is just a rewording or a longer/shorter version of the other.

Answer same=false when ANY of these hold:
- any load-bearing detail differs (weekly vs monthly, 10:00 vs 17:00, soft vs hard deadline, one recipient vs another),
- the two beliefs are about DIFFERENT specific things even if the surrounding pattern, complaint, or template is identical,
- the claims merely overlap, relate, or share a topic rather than being the same claim,
- or you are not confident.

The SPECIFIC THING each belief is ABOUT is load-bearing — different target means different belief:
- "hydration reminders are too frequent" ≠ "finger-stretch reminders are too frequent" (different reminder)
- "set the AC at 9 PM" ≠ "set the lights at 9 PM" (different device)
- the same-shaped preference about one person ≠ about a different person

Ignore phrasing, ordering, and extra incidental detail; the question is only whether — about the same specific thing — the core fact is one and the same. In `reason`, state what specific thing each belief targets and why they are or are not the identical claim.

When — and only when — same=true, also produce `canonical_statement`: the one statement that will REPLACE both. Carry over every load-bearing detail from each — if one is the fuller version, return the fuller one; if each adds something the other lacks, combine them faithfully into a single statement. Never drop a clause, qualifier, or instruction, and never invent anything neither states. When same=false, leave `canonical_statement` empty.
